> 
> ## Purple
> 
> Purple didn’t like bright lights.
> 
> Purple liked shadows, quiet corners, and secret places.
> 
> When Purple woke up, he slipped into the vents and under the floors, peeking out with wide eyes. Purple was shy — so shy that no one hardly ever saw him coming.
> 
> But Purple also liked to **test** people.
> 
> If someone walked by, Purple would wonder:
> “Will they notice me?”
> “Will they stop?”
> “Will they run?”
> 
> Purple didn’t want to be alone either. He just had a very creepy way of saying hello.
> 
> ## Yellow
> 
> Yellow came from the brightest tank of all.
> 
> Yellow had wings and loved flying through the dark ceilings of the park. Once, long ago, Yellow was meant to be the star of a sky ride for children — swooping, sparkling, and carrying lights overhead like a living parade balloon.
> 
> But after the storm, Yellow got mixed-up memories.
> 
> Now Yellow circled above, looking for the cheers and music that were supposed to fill the park.
> 
> When Yellow saw people, Yellow thought:
> “The show is starting!”
> 
> And then WHOOSH — down Yellow came, trying to scoop them up into the “ride.”
> 
> Of course, the people being chased did not feel like they were on a fun ride at all.
> 
> ## Why They Became Scary
> 
> At first, Mr. Red tried to teach them.
> 
> He told Blue, “Gentle.”
> He told Green, “Slowly.”
> He told Orange, “Wait.”
> He told Purple, “Don’t startle.”
> He told Yellow, “Too high!”
> 
> But something had gone wrong in the storm.
> 
> The Rainbow Friends were not evil.
> They were **mixed up**.
> 
> They had been made from colors, feelings, and bits of machine-thinking. Each one had one strong idea inside them, but not enough understanding to know how to behave around people.
> 
> - Blue wanted friends.
> - Green wanted to find someone.
> - Orange wanted food and energy.
> - Purple wanted attention.
> - Yellow wanted the show to begin.
> 
> But without anyone to guide them, all those feelings came out the wrong way.
